lesson:
```
struct Reading {
    int hour;
    double temp;
};

struct Reading r = {14, 31.5};
r.temp;                       /* 31.5 */
r.temp = 30.0;

struct Reading log[3] = {{6, 18.0}, {12, 27.5}, {18, 24.0}};
log[1].hour;                  /* 12 */
```

`typedef` gives the type a shorter name, so you can drop the word `struct`:

```
typedef struct { int hour; double temp; } Reading;
Reading r = {14, 31.5};
```

Through a pointer, use `->` (short for `(*p).temp`):

```
void cool(Reading *p) { p->temp -= 1.0; }
cool(&r);
```

Structs can be returned from functions and copied with `=`. This is how C programs (and Arduino libraries) organise data: a struct for the thing, plus functions that take it. C++ classes grew out of exactly this.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct { int hour; double temp; } Reading;

void cool(Reading *p) { p->temp -= 1.0; }

int main(void)
{
    Reading log[3] = {{6, 18.0}, {12, 27.5}, {18, 24.0}};
    cool(&log[1]);
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) printf("%02d:00  %.1f C\n", log[i].hour, log[i].temp);
    return 0;
}

